    <h2 class="center">What the NHS says about heat</h2>
    <p class="center lede">Not us. Published guidance, with the source so you can read it yourself.</p>
    <blockquote class="nhsq center">Heat applied to the affected area can be very effective in reducing pain.</blockquote>
    <p class="center" style="font-size:var(--t-sm);color:var(--ink-2)">nhs.uk, on easing joint stiffness at home</p>

    <div class="ev">
      <div class="ev-c"><p>Heat-pack guidance says apply warmth for 15 to 20 minutes. A hot water bottle holds useful warmth for 20 to 30 minutes, a microwaved wheat bag about 30. The dose in your cupboard is roughly right. It's the delivery that runs out.</p><cite>NHS and manufacturers' heat-pack guidance</cite></div>
      <div class="ev-c"><p>Knees, shoulders and elbows sit under thin skin with little muscle or fat around them, so they lose warmth faster than the rest of you and hold it worst. Cold hands, cold nose, cold knees.</p><cite class="todo" data-mock>Cite a named anatomy or physiology source here, or delete the citation line and let this stand as plain copy. A citation you can't produce is worse than none.</cite></div>
      <div class="ev-c"><p>NICE, which writes the guidance the NHS follows, puts exercise, weight management and information first for osteoarthritis. We're not a substitute for any of that. Warmth is what people use to get moving in the morning so they can do the rest.</p><cite>NICE guideline NG226 (2022). <a href="https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng226" rel="noopener">Read it</a></cite></div>
    </div>
    <p class="center" style="font-size:var(--t-sm);color:var(--ink-2);max-width:460px;margin:26px auto 0">Jointwell is a comfort device. It is not a medical treatment and does not cure, treat or prevent any condition. Keep doing what your GP and physio have told you to do.</p>

    <div class="cta-unit center">
